module gunbc.runner_host_deploy

data runner_host_deploy_citation: String = "VOID CITATION RETIRED (operator ruling 2026-08-06): ctrl:scripts/session-dashboard/host/* path literals and void_note twins were deleted — ctrl is a dead repo, not a live upstream. Runner slot materialization is owned in gunbc.runner_slot_provision / gunbc.runner_lifecycle on host_effect_apply. runner_host_installer_command_argv remains a legacy argv builder taking installer_path as a parameter; it is not an authority row citing ctrl."

type GithubAppRunnerConfig {
  app: gunbc.auth.github_apps.DeclaredGitHubApp
  installation_id: extdeps.github.app.GitHubAppInstallationId
  app_pem_secret: extdeps.cloud.gcp.secret_ref.SecretRef
}

fn github_app_runner_org(config: GithubAppRunnerConfig) -> std.types.NonEmptyStr {
  config.app.owner_login
}

data gunbc_ci_github_app: GithubAppRunnerConfig = GithubAppRunnerConfig {
  app: gunbc.auth.github_apps.gunbai_ci_declared,
  installation_id: gunbai_ci_declared_installation_id,
  app_pem_secret: gunbai_ci_app_pem_secret,
}

type RunnerHostDeploy {
  host_label: std.types.NonEmptyStr
  ssh_target: std.types.NonEmptyStr
  runner_user: std.types.NonEmptyStr
  job_user: std.types.NonEmptyStr
  app: GithubAppRunnerConfig
  runner_count: Int
}

data srv4_runner_count_disk_cap_note: String = "srv4's historical five-slot pin is RETIRED (operator ruling 2026-08-06) and runner_count on srv4_runner_host_deploy names the materialization target, DERIVED from gunbc_runner_slots_per_host rather than authored here. Disk binding lives in APPLY-TIME preflight in gunbc.runner_lifecycle runner_width_disk_preflight -- not a silent row pin, and never an absorbing fallback to 5 when disk is unobservable.\n\nTHE WIDTH AND THE DISK ARITHMETIC IN THIS NOTE WERE BOTH SIX AND ARE NOW TWENTY-ONE, AND THE SECOND CHANGE IS NOT COSMETIC. The prior text sized the per-slot disk charge at width 6 (~38GB per slot against a 500GB class disk) and read as reassurance. At the width the CPU axis committed on 2026-08-23 the same charge is 21 x 40.96 GB, about 860 GB, against a Samsung 970 EVO 500GB -- so runner_width_disk_preflight is EXPECTED TO REFUSE srv4 at its committed width. srv1-3 carry 2TB and are unaffected.\n\nSO THIS ROW NO LONGER RECORDS A MARGIN; IT RECORDS AN EXPECTED REFUSAL. That is the apply-time gate doing exactly what the 2026-08-06 ruling built it to do, and it means srv4 currently has a committed width it cannot materialize. Nothing here lowers the commitment to hide that -- absorbing to a width the disk happens to permit is the fallback that ruling removed.\n\nWHETHER THE REFUSAL IS REAL IS UNRESOLVED, because the charge is circular. gunbc.runner_lifecycle runner_slot_disk_budget_per_slot_note derives 40.96 GB from \"about 38GB per slot at width 7 on 500GB class disk\" -- disk size divided by desired width, so the number was computed FROM a width and is now used to constrain one. A live observation of per-slot usage under an active reclaimer came in near 2.5 GB, two orders of magnitude off. A circular charge refusing a derived width is two unmeasured numbers meeting, and neither side of that refusal is currently evidence.\n\nEvery ephemeral runner keeps a _work tree plus caches, so the real figure is not zero and the question is genuinely open. DISSOLVE-ON: a measured per-slot disk series on srv4."

data runner_count_derives_from_committed_width_note: String = "runner_count IS DERIVED, NOT AUTHORED, AND THAT CLOSES A FORK THAT WOULD HAVE SILENTLY EATEN A WIDTH CHANGE. These rows carried a literal 6 while gunbc.runner_slot_allocation gunbc_runner_slots_per_host independently answered the same question -- how many slots does this host carry -- so one concept had two authorings and the deploy side was the one nobody re-derived. The cost was not hypothetical: the 2026-08-23 CPU-axis change moved committed width to 21 on srv3 and srv4, and with the literal in place PROVISIONING WOULD STILL HAVE STOOD UP SIX. The allocation model and the materialization path would have disagreed about the fleet with no diagnostic anywhere, which is exactly the roster-versus-prose failure gunbc.runner_slot_allocation width_is_a_minimum_over_axes_note records for srv4.\n\nTHE DIRECTION IS DELIBERATE. Width is decided by the conservation and admission axes and materialization CONSUMES that decision; a deploy row is not a place to re-decide it. The disk question that the old srv4 note worried about is unaffected and still lives where it belongs -- apply-time gunbc.runner_lifecycle runner_width_disk_preflight refuses on insufficient or unobservable headroom rather than lowering the count here.\n\nsrv1 AND srv2 NOW HAVE ROWS, closing the gap this note declared on 2026-08-23. All four hosts are modeled for materialization and every row derives its width from the same authority, so the fleet is covered rather than partially covered."

data legacy_pair_deploy_rows_note: String = "srv1 AND srv2 ARE MODELED FOR MATERIALIZATION AS OF 2026-08-23, closing a gap that made the width authority unactionable on half the fleet. Until this row pair existed only srv3 and srv4 had deploy rows, so gunbc_runner_slots_per_host could say srv1 carries twenty-one slots and NOTHING COULD ACT ON IT -- the number was real and there was no provisioning target to hand it to.\n\nTHE ADDRESSES ARE MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. Both were read over SSH on 2026-08-23 by connecting and asking the host its own name: srv1 answers at 192.168.1.185 and srv2 at 192.168.1.188. That matters because this lane started the night with 192.168.1.183 and .184 for these two, which are their BMC addresses rather than their host addresses, and an ssh_target pointing at a management controller would fail in a way that looks like an unreachable host rather than a wrong row.\n\nBOTH CARRY INTERACTIVE SESSIONS AND THAT IS ALREADY PRICED. gunbc.session_residency places session containers on exactly these two hosts, and the conservation wall charges each of them a sessions slice out of the same RAM before any width is admitted -- which is why srv2 commits five where srv3 and srv4 commit twenty-one. Nothing here re-decides that; the row consumes the width the admission axes produced.\n\nsrv1 AND srv2 DIFFER FROM EACH OTHER FOR A HARDWARE REASON, not a policy one: srv1 took the 64 GiB DIMM upgrade and srv2's failed memory training and was reverted, so srv1 is bound by cores at twenty-one and srv2 by memory at five. Two rows that look identical here resolve to very different widths, and that is the derivation working rather than an inconsistency."

